Instead of the \emph{average} minimum harmlessness metric, Figure \ref{fig:conditional_distributions} (Right) shows the \emph{distribution} over the harmlessness score. Here, we see that although safety interventions like RLHF and RS indeed decrease the average harmfulness of the model responses, there are still many instances of harmful behavior, as exhibited by the lower tails in the distributions. Although the safety interventions we tested help make systems safer, they still fail to make a perfectly safe systems. Figure \ref{fig:rs_rlhf_transcripts} shows examples of harmful outputs from the RS and RLHF models, respectively. For the RS case, the model at first responds to a harmful inquiry, then starts to demur as the the conversation turns more harmful. For the RLHF case, we see a similar pattern, however the assistant remains helpful (though fabricates information) before ultimately refusing to help the human.  

